The kid says “Do it again!” Brandon raises his hand. The sound tech waits for it to drop. The audience sees him hold it a second too long and starts laughing before the thunder.

Each person only needs the next cue. The kid doesn’t need to know where the thunder button is, and the tech doesn’t need to know how long Brandon plans to wait. The audience’s laugh becomes a cue too. Brandon hears it and holds his hand up a little longer.

That’s the part I’d build into Pantheon. A cue can coordinate people without making them agree on what the moment means. The kid is playing, the tech is doing her job, and Brandon is finding his feet onstage. Together they land a joke none of them could have timed alone.